#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142717 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "489a2e3f-b599-5a48-8957-5f9f452b262e" :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting a Division Model"}] :question/id #uuid "ea196aed-62c9-50a9-aeab-2332278befad" :question/math-academy-id "e-2207" :question/problem "What division problem is represented by the model below?\n\n![](images/b1/b1cea7679d63c16d43e1f9b2f156cbd69057a6668fca2a4ee8bd868716631857.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{7}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{21}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/84/84cba43457adc42afc433238410fefc9fe98b042d326c10ea3fce6221e7e620c.png)\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{7} \\div 3 = \\dfrac{5}{21}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "15507c60-bead-5be3-8004-f96df661acfd" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Quotient From a Division Model"}] :question/id #uuid "386c19cf-1f88-551b-b1bc-6f4baa142d2a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2208" :question/problem "What picture is missing from the division model below?\n\n![](images/bb/bb9bf65df4eb34dac86c30479de00123c796c82d3137b69d20a1d21eecae67c3.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the model, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{6}\n$$\n\non the left.\n\nSince we need to divide by $\\color{blue}2$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/84/84f5fc54aa28636fd587cc712d039bdb984edec3dc3f5f22f891dbc1776fbd3e.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{12}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore, the missing picture is the following:\n\n![](images/a6/a68a88f55f3f06c1f238927ae53df6a64348e2ab190673e445568cd1161d46c7.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "46548510-66c1-542c-8a7b-a80d55247aa7" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Missing Divisor From a Division Model"}] :question/id #uuid "e85814d8-b93e-573e-aca5-2c64e601d05a" :question/math-academy-id "e-2209" :question/problem "What number is missing from the division problem below?\n\n![](images/0b/0b119e18348ad6f0ee31f2ad3e57cad4fc66403302df5590a8526ce39e930c78.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{20}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/eb/eb114471d69ebd728a176b521ab410ab2b16d2bccc039e003faacd9c5d445e96.png)\n\nThe division operation splits each vertical line into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts. So the shaded parts on the left are $\\color{blue}5$ times larger than the shaded parts on the right.\n\nTherefore, the division problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 3 4 \\div {\\color{blue}{5}} = \\dfrac{3}{20}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}5$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142720 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b44b363-0fb9-59c3-8fb6-8e83f3709f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing a Fraction by a Whole Number Using a Division Model"}] :question/id #uuid "c0f9c9b1-08f9-5ae6-8583-0b7f93904a18" :question/math-academy-id "e-2210" :question/problem "Use the model below to calculate $\\dfrac{5}{6} \\div 4$.\n\n![](images/d1/d1c1c3842b04174137bfe8a938c70493493b2c9a0e74bee619d965446c9326a6.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we need to divide by $\\color{blue}4$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/24/24bffed596b77ba53d57e04ebb6baede437d40f825c03a794ae7689f11c3c250.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{24}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6} \\div 4 = \\dfrac{5}{24} \\,\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186143499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186142945 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b44b363-0fb9-59c3-8fb6-8e83f3709f9e" :knowledge-point/